WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:10.332
Hello I'm Julie Theriot at Stanford University

00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:13.566
and I'm delighted to be taking part in this iBiology talk series

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:16.599
on great problems at the interface between physics and biology.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.899
The particular problem that I would like to explore with you today

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.832
is a question of how to go about discovering design principles for cells and organisms.

00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:28.399
This I think is a very fundamental question in biology

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.132
that reveals itself just when you look at any living organism.

00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:34.866
And I've showed you a few examples here at the cellular level,

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:38.466
where on the left we have one of Santiago Ramón y Cajal's

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.599
beautiful drawings of neurons in the brain.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:45.166
In the middle we have a light micrograph of an algae, Spyrogyra

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:48.699
where you can see the beautiful regular helical patterns of its chloroplasts.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.932
And over here on the right we have the little glass exoskeleton of a diatom,

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:56.832
which is a unicellular organism that's able to build itself

00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:58.966
this beautiful little glass house in which to live.

00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:04.966
Now if we were to see these kinds of very precise, robust and delicate structures

00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:08.032
in something it was built by human, we would assume that there was

00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:12.932
some sort of underlying design principle that the human had chosen to achieve some function,

00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:16.799
chosen materials and chosen the structural organization that would achieve that function.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.366
But at the level of cells, in the absence of an architect,

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:23.266
there have to be some other kind of underlying physical principles

00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:26.666
that enable this kind of organization to emerge.

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:32.099
Now we can think about this problem of a cellular self-organization at many different scales.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.832
And arguably one of the first interesting places where self organization happens

00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:38.766
in biological systems is just at the level of protein folding.

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.966
And I would actually argue that at this point

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:44.032
we basically understand pretty well how proteins fold up.

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:51.832
The interactions between the atoms in the protein as it’s folding are very familiar from

00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:57.332
fundamental physical and chemical principles and using just very basic equilibrium theory,

00:01:57.333 --> 00:02:01.899
it's possible to predict the folding of a protein with an astonishing degree of precision.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.966
Surely there are details left to understand

00:02:03.967 --> 00:02:06.132
but the physical principles I would say we do understand.

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:11.632
But as soon as we start to getting to larger scales then things all get to be a great big mess.

00:02:11.633 --> 00:02:14.732
So if you take a bunch of these proteins you put them together you make a cell.

00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:18.432
If you take a bunch of cells you put them together you can make a multi-cellular organisms

00:02:18.433 --> 00:02:20.932
such as this mouse embryo shown here.

00:02:20.933 --> 00:02:23.432
And if you take a bunch of organisms and put them together you can actually

00:02:23.433 --> 00:02:27.166
have very elaborate larger scale kinds of biological organization.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:31.966
One example I'm very fond of is this termite mountain shown here, which is several meters tall.

00:02:31.967 --> 00:02:37.066
And this enormous and elaborate structure with little nests and storage rooms down at the bottom

00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:40.232
and a big system of air conditioning built up top

00:02:40.233 --> 00:02:43.866
is put together by little termites that are only a few millimeters long.

00:02:43.867 --> 00:02:48.332
And I would postulate that there's not a termite architect is telling all the rest of them where to go.

00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:52.566
Instead they're able to use just local information and local behavioral rules

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:55.099
to put mud together to build this big structure.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:58.199
I think that's a very good analogy for what must be happening

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.099
at the level of the organism and at the level of the cell.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.399
So fundamentally as we try to understand this level from physical principles

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.166
our equilibrium theories that worked pretty well for protein folding just failed dramatically

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:13.432
at all higher scales of organization.

00:03:13.433 --> 00:03:17.932
So one way maybe for asking this question about how we're going to discover design principles,

00:03:17.933 --> 00:03:21.166
is to say in the context of protein folding, for example,

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.699
we have a privilege state - the equilibrium state.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:29.232
But what equilibrium would that be for something that is like a cell or like an organism

00:03:29.233 --> 00:03:31.566
with no obvious global privilege state

00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:35.632
and yet is able to generate robust, reproducible and precise forms.

00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:38.299
Now that's a difficult question.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:42.532
And I think it becomes even more difficult and even more puzzling when you realize that

00:03:42.533 --> 00:03:47.766
of course almost all biological structures are not only precisely defined but also are dynamic.

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:52.599
There's one case where you can see this very clearly which is cell motility.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.332
Now there many different kinds of cells, unicellular organisms

00:03:56.333 --> 00:03:58.999
or cells within multicellular organisms such as humans,

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.066
that are able to crawl around in a directed and purposeful way.

00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:06.832
And one of the most motile cells we work on a great deal in my group is shown here.

00:04:06.833 --> 00:04:12.032
These are cells from the skin of a fish. And their structural elements can be highlighted.

00:04:12.033 --> 00:04:15.366
So for example in this image by my students Sunny Lou.

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:18.332
She has used a fluorescently labeled mushroom toxin to

00:04:18.333 --> 00:04:22.499
highlight the structures of all of the actin filaments within this particular cell.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.132
So particular over here in this movie that was made by a former student Patricia Yam.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:31.266
She is using essentially the same technique to build up a little bit of texture

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:34.066
in the podium inside this living cell.

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:36.066
So as a cell starts to crawl,

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:40.799
what I hope you can appreciate is that the net movement of the cell arises

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.732
because the mesh work is assembled at the front or at the top,

00:04:43.733 --> 00:04:45.866
and that is disassembled at the back.

00:04:45.867 --> 00:04:50.199
But remarkably those two rates are almost exactly identical to one another.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.832
Because the cell moves forward without changing its top to bottom length.

00:04:53.833 --> 00:04:57.632
Similarly the rate of mesh work assembly over on the right side of the cell

00:04:57.633 --> 00:05:00.866
is got to be the same as the rate of mesh work assembly over on the left side

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:03.099
so it is able to move forward without turning.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:08.866
So how is it that the cell is able to put together all these very large scale structural elements

00:05:08.867 --> 00:05:13.566
that are constantly turning over and yet do so with such precisely balanced rates?

00:05:13.567 --> 00:05:16.566
How does it know how to make the shape?

00:05:16.567 --> 00:05:19.799
Well we don't know the answer to that.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.832
But I hope I can at least take a step back and focus on a slightly simpler question or

00:05:23.833 --> 00:05:27.666
what should be a slightly simpler question to challenge you with this idea

00:05:27.667 --> 00:05:32.732
of trying to come up with physical principles that will enable cells to do things like measure length,

00:05:32.733 --> 00:05:35.832
and count things and measure spacings as they are building structures.

00:05:35.833 --> 00:05:39.832
And in particular I'd like to focus on a relatively simple case,

00:05:39.833 --> 00:05:44.432
which is when cells build structures with filaments wrapped by membrane

00:05:44.433 --> 00:05:46.932
that protrude outward from their surface.

00:05:46.933 --> 00:05:50.732
There are many examples in biology where cells are able to build these kinds of

00:05:50.733 --> 00:05:54.766
surface protrusions that clearly have defined and measured length.

00:05:54.767 --> 00:05:58.932
So for example over on the left this is an electron micrographs,

00:05:58.933 --> 00:06:02.599
a cross section of the surface of an intestinal epithelial cell.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.732
We see it's covered with these little fingers that are sticking up.

00:06:05.733 --> 00:06:06.999
They're all wrapped in membrane

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.466
and that enable an increase of surface area for the absorption of nutrients.

00:06:11.467 --> 00:06:16.266
Now in the middle we have a scanning electron micrograph of a ciliate.

00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:19.132
And this is a single cell organism that looks all hairy.

00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:23.499
Each of these individual little hairs is a beating cilium that the cell is using to swim around.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:27.832
And you can see both on the intestinal epithelium and on the cilia

00:06:27.833 --> 00:06:30.932
that all of these many hundreds of different surface protrusions

00:06:30.933 --> 00:06:34.999
are of really pretty much exactly the same length so it has to have some way to measure that.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.432
Now maybe even more interesting case that I'll come back to

00:06:38.433 --> 00:06:42.832
is the stereocilia on the surface of the hair cell in the inner ear.

00:06:42.833 --> 00:06:46.632
And this little cluster of surface protrusions is what enables us to

00:06:46.633 --> 00:06:50.566
perceive motions in the air as sound within our brains.

00:06:50.567 --> 00:06:55.766
And you can see in this case that not only are these stereocilia in perfectly well defined length

00:06:55.767 --> 00:07:00.466
but in fact different stereocillia on the surface of the same cell all have different length.

00:07:00.467 --> 00:07:03.632
And they organize themselves in this incredibly precise

00:07:03.633 --> 00:07:07.266
staircase step pattern like a set of organ pipes.

00:07:07.267 --> 00:07:12.899
So we want to be able to come up with some proposition of general organizational principles

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.566
that can allow these kinds of structures to be built by cells.

00:07:15.567 --> 00:07:18.532
And in order to do that we need to know a little bit more about their structure.

00:07:18.533 --> 00:07:23.199
In particular all of these surface protrusions are made by structural filaments

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.632
that are bundled together and then wrapped around by membrane.

00:07:26.633 --> 00:07:31.166
These filaments themselves are also assembled from smaller subunits.

00:07:31.167 --> 00:07:33.366
And in the cases that we are going to be talking about,

00:07:33.367 --> 00:07:38.899
these filaments are actually self assembled by multiple different copies of identical subunits.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.132
And your first approximation, the same sort of simple equilibrium theories

00:07:43.133 --> 00:07:45.399
that enable us to describe protein folding,

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:49.466
can also enable us to describe these very simple sorts of protein-protein interactions,

00:07:49.467 --> 00:07:52.199
that build in this case helical filaments

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:56.132
which as many copies of the same subunit interacting over and over again.

00:07:56.133 --> 00:08:01.499
In the simple systems we can also precisely measure aspects of their rate, of their turnover.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.599
And so for actin filaments that we are focused on today

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.699
there been a couple of really very beautiful experiments

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.999
measuring precisely all of the rates that govern their self assembly and behavior.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.566
So for example in this one very famous study from Tom Pollard done in 1986.

00:08:17.567 --> 00:08:22.032
He was able to measure the on-rate and the off-rate of individual subunits

00:08:22.033 --> 00:08:27.699
landing on the ends of the filaments, both on the two structurally distinct ends

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:31.766
and also as a function of whether the subunits contain ATP or ADP.

00:08:31.767 --> 00:08:34.066
So all of those numbers are known.

00:08:34.067 --> 00:08:37.499
Okay so if you have a system like this where you have a bunch of protein subunits

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:38.766
that are able to self-associate,

00:08:38.767 --> 00:08:41.899
is that something that just by itself can give you a well defined length?

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:45.832
Well the answer actually is no. It's very clearly no.

00:08:45.833 --> 00:08:49.099
It's been shown experimentally and also described theoretically

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.366
that if you have this kind of self assembling subunits

00:08:51.367 --> 00:08:56.532
then that the default length distribution is going to be exponential.

00:08:56.533 --> 00:08:58.732
The fundamental reason for that is that

00:08:58.733 --> 00:09:02.066
each little subunit doesn't know what the rest of the film is doing.

00:09:02.067 --> 00:09:06.399
It's behavior is governed just by these local rates, these local interactions

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:10.299
but because each one of those additional lots of reactions is essentially independent,

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:12.499
the exponential distribution is the default.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:15.532
So we can get some aspects of filament structure

00:09:15.533 --> 00:09:18.566
in particular its diameter and also its dynamics

00:09:18.567 --> 00:09:22.232
defined by just the structure of the protein subunits that make it up.

00:09:22.233 --> 00:09:26.732
But for length, if we want the length to be some precisely defined number,

00:09:26.733 --> 00:09:29.599
there has to be some other mechanism for determining that.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:35.166
Now of course inside of living cells, filamentous proteins never operate on their own.

00:09:35.167 --> 00:09:37.432
There are always lots and lots of other proteins,

00:09:37.433 --> 00:09:41.366
in fact hundreds or possibly thousands of other proteins that interact

00:09:41.367 --> 00:09:44.632
with these cytoskeletal filament structures in order to determine their behavior.

00:09:44.633 --> 00:09:49.399
And this is a textbook illustration of just a few of the different kinds of proteins

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:52.366
that affect cytoskeletal filaments, and the details here aren't important

00:09:52.367 --> 00:09:57.799
but the basic idea is just for every aspect of dynamics or structures of these filamentous proteins

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:02.366
there are some other protein that is able to interact with those subunits and regulate their behavior.

00:10:02.367 --> 00:10:07.699
So there are other proteins that can regulate the on-rate and the off-rate of the subunits at the ends

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:10.232
that can bind to the filaments and regulate their stability,

00:10:10.233 --> 00:10:13.499
they can force the elements to bundle together etc, etc.

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:17.566
Okay so now if we know that there are other proteins

00:10:17.567 --> 00:10:20.366
that will interact with these filament forming proteins.

00:10:20.367 --> 00:10:23.599
We go back to our problem of how do you set a defined length.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.532
You might imagine that a really easy way to do it would be just to have a protein

00:10:27.533 --> 00:10:30.066
whose job it is to determine that length.

00:10:30.067 --> 00:10:33.966
And in fact there are some cases in biology where we know that that happens.

00:10:33.967 --> 00:10:37.466
One very well characterized case is in bacteriophage lambda.

00:10:37.467 --> 00:10:39.632
So this is a kind of virus that infects bacteria.

00:10:39.633 --> 00:10:43.799
And it makes these spectacular little sort of landing pods structures.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:48.899
And there is a tail that is part of the structure where if you look at that tail

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:52.932
in many different individual virus particles, as you can see in the micrograph,

00:10:52.933 --> 00:10:54.566
those tails are all the same length.

00:10:54.567 --> 00:11:00.032
And it's been shown that there is a protein called H protein, a big long protein

00:11:00.033 --> 00:11:02.032
whose length determines the length of that tail.

00:11:02.033 --> 00:11:06.466
Internal inframe deletions that make the protein shorter also make the tail shorter

00:11:06.467 --> 00:11:10.799
and duplications that make the protein longer also make the tail longer.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:14.466
So that's great but it doesn't have to be a protein that acts as the template

00:11:14.467 --> 00:11:16.132
it could be a different sort of macromolecule.

00:11:16.133 --> 00:11:19.999
And in fact there are other viruses such as tobacco mosaic virus shown here

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.466
that instead of using a protein to determine their length,

00:11:22.467 --> 00:11:24.832
they use the RNA genome to determine the length.

00:11:24.833 --> 00:11:26.232
But the idea is still the same.

00:11:26.233 --> 00:11:28.866
If you have a template that's a great way to solve this problem.

00:11:28.867 --> 00:11:31.066
But of course we're not done.

00:11:31.067 --> 00:11:35.599
Because the bottom line is this really simple elegant idea of having a template

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:38.899
simply doesn't work in any of those cases that I showed you,

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:42.799
in the intestinal microvilli, in the cilia on the surface of the swimming organism

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:45.332
or in the sterocilia on the surface of the hair cell.

00:11:45.333 --> 00:11:48.166
So what determines length in those kinds of cases?

00:11:48.167 --> 00:11:51.366
It's got to be something that's dynamic.

00:11:51.367 --> 00:11:55.766
So let's focus in particular on these hair cell stereocilia

00:11:55.767 --> 00:11:59.799
because I think this is just an exquisitely beautiful piece of biology

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:02.666
that's actually filled with really really interesting question and physics.

00:12:02.667 --> 00:12:06.899
Now the organization of the stereocilia as I showed you before

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:12.666
is in bundles where each individual protrusion is a defined length

00:12:12.667 --> 00:12:16.332
and they're organized in such a way that the protrusions lined up

00:12:16.333 --> 00:12:19.299
so that they're each next to a neighbor that's slightly longer.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:21.366
And the reason why that's important

00:12:21.367 --> 00:12:26.066
is because every pair of neighboring filaments of different length in the structure

00:12:26.067 --> 00:12:29.332
is attached by a little structure called a tip link.

00:12:29.333 --> 00:12:35.699
And when these hair cells in the inner ear have the tips of their those bundles bent

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:40.199
by the movements of membranes that react essentially to fluid flow

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:43.966
that comes downstream of the bouncing around the eardrum.

00:12:43.967 --> 00:12:47.766
Then the movement of those stereocilia relative to one another

00:12:47.767 --> 00:12:53.532
causes the slightly longer stereocillia to slide, with respect to slightly shorter neighbor.

00:12:53.533 --> 00:12:57.866
And so tugging on that tip link then opens ion channels

00:12:57.867 --> 00:12:59.966
that enable changes in membrane polarization,

00:12:59.967 --> 00:13:02.732
which can eventually be perceived as an electrical signal

00:13:02.733 --> 00:13:04.232
and interpreted as a sound by the brain.

00:13:04.233 --> 00:13:08.266
Okay so it's obvious that it's very important that

00:13:08.267 --> 00:13:12.166
these stereocilia be built a certain length in a certain pattern on the surface of the cell.

00:13:12.167 --> 00:13:15.766
And also actually it turns out that they need be tuned

00:13:15.767 --> 00:13:19.066
to different frequencies so that you can hear both high pitches and low pitches.

00:13:19.067 --> 00:13:23.632
And I want to share with you just an absolutely astonishing study

00:13:23.633 --> 00:13:27.366
that was done by Lewis Tilney and his colleagues back in the 1980s,

00:13:27.367 --> 00:13:29.732
that I think shows the magnitude of this problem

00:13:29.733 --> 00:13:32.999
and how difficult it really is going to be to try to understand how it works.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:40.132
What they did here was simple but extremely painstaking series of experiments

00:13:40.133 --> 00:13:43.399
where they dissected out the cochlea from newborn checks.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.899
And then use scanning electron microscopy to

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.232
examine the hair cells in different parts of the cochlea.

00:13:48.233 --> 00:13:51.832
So one end there are hair cells that are tuned to hear high frequencies

00:13:51.833 --> 00:13:54.332
at the other end hair cells tuned to hear low frequencies

00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:57.566
and then graded frequency determination in between.

00:13:57.567 --> 00:14:01.199
What they found is if they look at the end that can hear high frequencies

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:04.466
they found these short, little, stiff, stubby stereocilia.

00:14:04.467 --> 00:14:08.266
But if they look at the end of the cochlea that was to hear long frequencies

00:14:08.267 --> 00:14:11.366
they found these long, elegant sort of floppy stereocilia.

00:14:11.367 --> 00:14:14.866
Okay that's pretty cool but the really amazing part

00:14:14.867 --> 00:14:18.499
is then they went ahead and measure the total number of actin filaments,

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:19.632
the total length of those filaments,

00:14:19.633 --> 00:14:22.432
the total surface area membrane that wrapped around all these things

00:14:22.433 --> 00:14:24.832
for all these different cells throughout the cochlea.

00:14:24.833 --> 00:14:28.299
It turned out that those numbers were absolutely conserved.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:32.199
So there were 9,000 microns of actin filaments

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:34.832
making up each of these very different kinds of structures.

00:14:34.833 --> 00:14:37.232
There's 180 square microns of membrane

00:14:37.233 --> 00:14:39.799
that's wrapped around each of these very different kinds of structures.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:45.899
And yet each individual cell tunes the overall length number and spacing of these stereocilia

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.932
to be able to hear the pitch that it's intended to hear.

00:14:48.933 --> 00:14:52.066
So this means is that you know every single one of the cells

00:14:52.067 --> 00:14:53.799
basically start with the same Lego kit.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.632
It had the same number of structural proteins and the same amount of membrane

00:14:56.633 --> 00:14:59.966
And yet each cell was to arrange them in slightly different ways

00:14:59.967 --> 00:15:03.066
depending on where it was in the cochlea and what pitch it wants to hear.

00:15:03.067 --> 00:15:06.066
Now we don't understand how that works.

00:15:06.067 --> 00:15:10.799
Not only do we not understand in detail how the length of these structures can be set

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:14.399
but we also don't understand how those length can be so finely tuned

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:19.832
in order to achieve this kind of biological specificity.

00:15:19.833 --> 00:15:24.132
Now that's just one example, but I think it opens up this huge can of worms

00:15:24.133 --> 00:15:28.799
about how we go about understanding cell structure determination.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:34.166
And I think this is a subset of the very general question raised by Schrödinger of what is life.

00:15:34.167 --> 00:15:37.332
Certainly life has to obey physical and chemical principles

00:15:37.333 --> 00:15:40.732
but there are additional principles that I believe we have not yet discovered

00:15:40.733 --> 00:15:43.132
that are necessary to describe these kinds of processes

00:15:43.133 --> 00:15:45.199
that we see inside of living cells and organisms.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:47.666
And cells are very complicated.

00:15:47.667 --> 00:15:50.166
The inside of the cell was extremely crowded

00:15:50.167 --> 00:15:52.332
as you can see in this election micrograph reconstruction.

00:15:52.333 --> 00:15:57.032
It's very inhomogeneous. Everything is constantly far away from equilibrium,

00:15:57.033 --> 00:15:59.132
and its material properties are very weird.

00:15:59.133 --> 00:16:03.232
It's not viscous, it's not elastic, it's not plastic, it's some combination of all those things

00:16:03.233 --> 00:16:06.032
and actually can change its material properties over time.

00:16:06.033 --> 00:16:10.632
So with all this kind of weirdness and complexity in strange material properties

00:16:10.633 --> 00:16:12.899
how are the cells nevertheless able to build these

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:16.066
exquisitely regular beautiful structures that we see all the time.

00:16:16.067 --> 00:16:19.666
Well I think it's very clear that equilibrium thermodynamics

00:16:19.667 --> 00:16:22.066
and statistical mechanics are not gonna get us there.

00:16:22.067 --> 00:16:24.666
It's a good first step, but it's not going to really be able to

00:16:24.667 --> 00:16:29.432
describe the physical principles for cell organization in a living, in a real living system.

00:16:29.433 --> 00:16:34.132
And I'd like to propose that one way to really try to get at this

00:16:34.133 --> 00:16:35.966
and to start building a general theory of the cell

00:16:35.967 --> 00:16:39.099
is to start with questions that seem like they might be relatively simple.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:42.966
How do cells measure things?  How do cell count things?

00:16:42.967 --> 00:16:45.932
The hope is that once we understand the principles

00:16:45.933 --> 00:16:48.432
underlying those kinds of relatively simple processes,

00:16:48.433 --> 00:16:51.032
we should be able to put them together to understand

00:16:51.033 --> 00:16:54.932
robust and dynamic cellular organization also in more complex contexts.

00:16:54.933 --> 00:16:58.232
In complex context for the life of the individual cell

00:16:58.233 --> 00:17:02.066
and also in order to build a whole multicellular organisms such as ourselves.

00:17:02.067 --> 00:17:05.766
And at some point I hope that if we understand these physical principles

00:17:05.767 --> 00:17:07.632
and these organizational principles well enough,

00:17:07.633 --> 00:17:10.199
then we should be able to also engineer them.

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:13.666
And be able to get the cells to build structures that we choose,

00:17:13.667 --> 00:17:43.700
rather than just building the structures that they choose.

